motivation:
\label{sec:methods-motivation}

Classical studies in cognitive vision and psychology demonstrate that underlying 3D geometry and orientations can be inferred from 2D visual contours \citep{richards1987inferring} and compared through mental rotation \citep{shepard1971mental}. Inspired by these foundational insights, we formulate 3D orientation alignment as a projection-matching task: rotating a 3D object until its 2D silhouette matches a target image. Restricting physical interaction to 3D rotation provides two key methodological advantages. First, out-of-plane rotations induce nonlinear silhouette deformations, aspect changes, and self-occlusion, creating an inherently visual challenge that resists textual representation. Second, pure rotation cleanly decouples spatial orientation reasoning from extraneous physical dynamics—such as translational drift, grasp affordances, and contact friction—allowing us to examine whether models can turn 2D observations into 3D understanding that supports precise action.

To reduce reliance on semantic shortcuts and encourage visual-spatial reasoning, RoTA-Bench synthesizes 3D objects from 2D vector icons rather than drawing directly from common 3D CAD repositories, where familiar shapes may trigger pre-trained priors. By extruding 2D icons within a spherical volume, this automated generation produces abstract, novel 3D geometries while mathematically guaranteeing that the canonical front view reproduces the original icon, preserving its fine-grained details (Appendix~\ref{app:target-orientation}). Finally, generating initial poses via sequences of reversible discrete rotations guarantees by construction that every task is solvable.

task:
\label{sec:methods-task-definition}

Following the design rationale in Section~\ref{sec:methods-motivation}, we formulate the interactive projection-matching task as follows. Each task instance is defined by a rigid 3D object $G \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ centered at the coordinate origin, an initial pose $R_0 \in \mathrm{SO}(3)$, and a canonical target pose $R^\star = I_3$. Let $\Pi(\cdot)$ denote the orthographic projection operator that renders the binary silhouette mask of object $G$ at a given pose. At step $t$, the environment provides an observation triplet:
\begin{equation}
O_t = (I^\star,\, V_t,\, I_t), \quad I^\star = \Pi(G, R^\star), \quad I_t = \Pi(G, R_t),
\label{eq:observation-tuple}
\end{equation}
where $I^\star \in \{0, 1\}^{H \times W}$ represents the invariant target silhouette, and $I_t \in \{0, 1\}^{H \times W}$ is the current projection silhouette. To complement the binary silhouette with missing depth and occlusion cues, the agent also receives a depth-shaded auxiliary view $V_t \in [0, 255]^{H \times W}$, rendered from the identical viewpoint as $I_t$.

Conditioned on this visual feedback, the agent interacts with the object through a discrete action space. At each step $t$, the agent selects an action $a_t$ from a vocabulary $\mathcal{A}$ consisting of 24 camera-fixed rotation actions and a terminal STOP command. These 24 actions span six directional senses across three principal camera axes---pitch (up/down), yaw (left/right), and in-plane roll (clockwise/counterclockwise)---paired with four step sizes $\Delta\theta \in \{1^\circ, 5^\circ, 10^\circ, 30^\circ\}$ to enable both coarse and fine pose adjustments. Letting $Q(a_t) \in \mathrm{SO}(3)$ denote the rotation matrix for action $a_t$, the object pose updates via:
\begin{equation}
R_{t+1} = Q(a_t) R_t.
\label{eq:state-transition}
\end{equation}
Executing $a_t$ updates the 3D state and re-renders observations $I_{t+1}$ and $V_{t+1}$.

To evaluate projection alignment, we compute the projection Intersection over Union (projection IoU) between the current projection $I_t$ and the target silhouette $I^\star$:
\begin{equation}
s_t = \operatorname{IoU}(I_t, I^\star) = \frac{|I_t \cap I^\star|}{|I_t \cup I^\star|}.
\label{eq:projection-iou}
\end{equation}
An episode succeeds whenever $s_t \ge \tau$, where we set $\tau = 0.90$. This threshold enforces high visual alignment fidelity while providing a necessary tolerance margin for the discrete action space, where exact alignment is difficult to achieve in finite steps. Furthermore, because geometric symmetries and orthographic self-overlap can produce identical silhouettes from distinct 3D poses, the task naturally admits multiple equivalent target poses. We formalize this goal set as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{goal}}(G, I^\star) = \{ R \in \mathrm{SO}(3) \mid \operatorname{IoU}(\Pi(G, R), I^\star) \ge \tau \}.
\label{eq:goal-set}
\end{equation}
Finally, an episode terminates upon task success ($s_t \ge \tau$), voluntary halting via STOP, reaching a maximum action budget, or stagnation after consecutive steps without improving the best IoU.

construction:
\label{sec:methods-dataset}

Our automated pipeline constructs the benchmark across three sequential phases: task generation, reference search, and dataset construction, as summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:formal-pipeline}.

First, in the task generation phase, following the design rationale in Section~\ref{sec:methods-motivation}, we curate 2D vector icons from the open-source Game-icons library (Appendix~\ref{app:icon-sources}), rasterizing them into high-resolution binary masks after geometric filtering on connectivity, occupancy, aspect ratio, and foreground point count (Appendix~\ref{app:icon-processing}). To construct a 3D object from each 2D icon while strictly bounding its spatial profile under arbitrary rotations, the planar point set $P \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ is scaled within a disk of radius $r$ and symmetrically extruded along the depth axis under a spherical envelope:
\begin{equation}
h(x, y) = \sqrt{\max(r^2 - x^2 - y^2,\, 0)}, \quad z \in [-h(x, y),\, h(x, y)].
\label{eq:depth-envelope}
\end{equation}
The resulting solid $G = \{(x, y, z) \mid (x, y) \in P,\, |z| \le h(x, y)\}$ is discretized as a dense 3D point cloud (Appendix~\ref{app:icon-processing}). Moreover, because 3D rotations preserve Euclidean norms, any rotated pose satisfies $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le r^2$. This spherical invariance prevents viewport clipping and fixes the camera scale globally across all tasks. To provide monocular depth cues and bridge discretization gaps between sampled points, visual observations are rendered via orthographic projection using depth-dependent shading and Gaussian smoothing (Appendix~\ref{app:icon-processing}).

To preserve the original 2D icon's fine contour details (Appendix~\ref{app:target-orientation}), the canonical target pose is fixed to $R^\star = I_3$, and initial poses $R_0 = Q(b_L) \cdots Q(b_1) R^\star$ are generated via $L \sim \text{Uniform}\{6, \dots, 16\}$ discrete rotations. At each step, $b_k \in \mathcal{A}_{30^\circ}$ rotates by $30^\circ$, with its direction sampled uniformly at random from the six candidate camera directions (each with probability $1/6$), forbidding the immediate inverse ($b_k \ne b_{k-1}^{-1}$) to avoid cancellations and guarantee genuine displacement. Initial poses already projection-aligned ($s_0 \ge \tau$) are discarded. Crucially, because every rotation in $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ is invertible, executing the reversed sequence $Q(b_1)^{-1} \cdots Q(b_L)^{-1}$ restores $R^\star$, ensuring 100\% reachability and task solvability by construction. In total, this generation pipeline yields 674 canonical tasks with unique source icons.

Second, in the reference search phase, we compute reference paths using pose-guided beam search and $30^\circ$-restricted breadth-first search. These discover significantly shorter trajectories than raw reversed generation paths (Appendix~\ref{app:search-reference}).

Finally, in the dataset construction phase, a stratified test set of 100 tasks was frozen across reference path lengths ($\ge 4$ steps) using proportional stratified sampling via Hamilton's method and deterministic hash sorting, reserving the remaining 574 tasks for future training and research (Appendix~\ref{app:dataset-construction}).

metrics:
\label{sec:methods-metrics}

\paragraph{Task performance metrics.}
Task Success Rate is the fraction of episodes reaching IoU $s_t\geq0.90$. Mean Final IoU and mean rotation steps average the final IoU and executed rotations over all tasks, including failures. An episode fails if it ends through STOP, the 48-decision limit, or stagnation before reaching the threshold.

\paragraph{Geometric diagnostic metrics.}
We compute 3D pose error from ground-truth pose data, which are not provided to the model:
\begin{equation}
e_t = \min_{S \in \mathcal{G}} d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}(R_t,\, R^\star S) = \min_{S \in \mathcal{G}} \arccos\left(\frac{\operatorname{Tr}(R_t (R^\star S)^\top) - 1}{2}\right).
\label{eq:pose-error}
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathcal{G}$ contains the object's estimated symmetries (Appendix~\ref{app:object-symmetries}). Pose error is the smallest rotation needed to reach an equivalent target pose. Equivalent target poses produce exact alignment, whereas task success requires only a projection IoU of at least $0.90$. Thus, a successful pose need not have zero pose error. Pose error is used as a geometric diagnostic rather than as the task objective; a lower pose error does not necessarily imply a larger immediate IoU improvement. Surface coverage is the fraction of the object's surface observed during an episode.

A direction (axis and sign) is effective if at least one legal angle reduces pose error: $\min_{\theta\in\{1^\circ,5^\circ,10^\circ,30^\circ\}}e(R_t,d,\theta)<e_t$. Effective Direction Rate is the fraction of rotations that choose such a direction; the chosen angle may still increase error. For effective directions, let $\Theta_t^\star=\arg\min_\theta e(R_t,d,\theta)$ be the best legal angles. Best Legal Angle Rate is the fraction choosing one of these angles. Angle Gap is $\min_{\theta\in\Theta_t^\star}|\theta_{\mathrm{selected}}-\theta|$. Angle Loss is the extra pose error caused by the chosen angle: $e(R_t,d,\theta_{\mathrm{selected}})-\min_\theta e(R_t,d,\theta)$.

We also compare changes in IoU and pose error, and correlate rotation angles with both values before each action. Appendix~\ref{app:finding-evidence} gives the definitions and confidence intervals.

association:
\label{sec:results-guidance}

We next examine whether rotation-angle choices rely more on visible projection overlap or on the underlying 3D pose discrepancy. Figure~\ref{fig:all-method-correlations} and Appendix Table~\ref{tab:all-method-correlations} report their correlations with pre-action IoU and pose error. For GPT-5.6-Sol, the correlation is stronger with IoU than with pose error ($\rho=-0.536$, 95\% CI $[-0.617,-0.443]$ vs.\ $\rho=0.368$, 95\% CI $[0.166,0.532]$). Human angles are strongly associated with both ($\rho=-0.696$, 95\% CI $[-0.763,-0.633]$, and $\rho=0.751$, 95\% CI $[0.675,0.828]$). Most evaluated models show the same relative pattern as GPT-5.6-Sol. These results suggest that most models rely more on projection overlap when choosing rotation angles, whereas humans use both projection overlap and 3D pose discrepancy.

To conduct a comparative analysis under the same conditions as closely as possible, we examine action consequences across the 211 exact state pairs shared by Human and GPT-5.6-Sol, where direct visual coverage before the matched actions is close (42.01\% vs.\ 46.21\%, Figure~\ref{fig:shared-state-context} and Appendix Table~\ref{tab:shared_state_context}). We find that image-space and pose-space progress can diverge even when actions start from the same pose. Humans execute substantially more DC actions (33.18\% vs.\ 23.70\%), showing a willingness to accept transient drops in 2D overlap to reduce underlying 3D pose error. Conversely, GPT-5.6-Sol executes nearly twice as many UF actions as humans (19.43\% vs.\ 10.90\%), showing a greater willingness to accept increases in 3D pose error to improve 2D overlap. For the remaining outcomes, humans achieve far higher UC actions (50.24\% vs.\ 34.60\%) and avoid DF moves (5.69\% vs.\ 22.27\%). Together, these results show that image-space and pose-space progress can diverge: at matched states, GPT-5.6-Sol more often improves silhouette overlap while worsening pose alignment than humans.

ablation:
\label{sec:results-ablation}

We evaluate the contribution of visual depth cues and multi-turn feedback by ablating the auxiliary depth view and interaction history for GPT-5.6-Sol across the first 30 tasks, whose distribution of reference solution steps strongly aligns with the 100-task test set (Spearman's $\rho = 0.9121$, $p = 3.56 \times 10^{-5}$; 10,000 bootstrap resamples yield a 95\% CI lower bound of $0.7445$). As reported in Table~\ref{tab:input_ablation}, removing the auxiliary view reduces task success from 16.67\% (full input) to 3.33\% and final IoU from 0.5656 to 0.5490. Removing interaction history reduces observed success from 5/30 tasks to 0/30 and final IoU from 0.5656 to 0.4822, close to uniform random performance (0.4808). The observed effect of removing history is larger than that of removing the auxiliary view.

Interestingly, across ablation settings, the effective-direction rate remains nearly unchanged (54.85\%--56.52\%), while angle selection changes markedly. Removing the auxiliary depth view increases $30^\circ$ rotations to 86.14\% but leaves the IoU--angle correlation close to that under full input ($\rho=-0.463$ vs.\ $-0.472$). Without interaction history, $30^\circ$ rotations rise to 96.76\% from 62.75\%, $1^\circ$ adjustments fall to 0.16\% from 12.18\%, and the correlation weakens to $\rho=-0.242$. In this ablation, history helps the model choose how far to rotate more than which way to rotate. Previous views and actions may help it recognize when to follow a coarse turn with a finer adjustment. Counts and paired intervals are in Appendix~\ref{app:finding-two}. These results reveal that GPT-5.6-Sol's fine-grained control is sensitive to interaction history.
